# Design review — enforced critique protocol

Applies to **every** change to user-visible surfaces — `app/ui/` and
`app/ui_admin/` source files (.js/.css/.html/.j2, excluding generated
bundles/manifests). The `.devin/hooks.v1.json` Stop hook
(`stop-gate.mjs`, design-review + undefined-token checks) refuses to close a
UI-touching session without the artifact below. This rule is why the gate
exists — read it, the hook only enforces its shape.

## Why this rule exists (the failure it kills)

Three documented failure moves forced this into a hard gate:

1. **Checklist substitution.** The ask was "is this good design / easy to
   use"; the work done was "does it render, is the data real." A self-set
   easier question was answered and reported against the user's bar.
2. **Verification-as-critique.** "Zero console errors" and "element
   present" were reported as design judgments. Mechanical checks verify
   mechanics — never quality.
3. **Self-authored pass criteria.** Review gates were written to measure
   what the checker intended to check (e.g. "G-VISUAL: zero console
   errors"), so the gate could never fail on design.
4. **The chef never tastes the food.** A full polish pass produced
   findings, fixed them, repainted, and reported "verified" — where
   verified meant *the fixes appear on camera*. The flat surfaces (an
   empty date-hero slab, a detail page that is a stack of form rows)
   sailed through because the pass only asked "is it broken," never
   "does it look deliberately composed." Mechanical verification of a
   fix is not a judgment of the surface — and a verdict that never
   happened cannot be caught. The verdict must therefore be a required
   artifact: written, per surface, falsifiable.

The remedy is structural: the design judgment must happen in its own pass,
produce an auditable artifact, and happen before fixes are written — not
be folded into "did my change appear on screen."

## The protocol — three passes (the re-read precedes the render)

### Pass 0 — Read-back (before the first render)

After writing and before any screenshot: re-read the file top to
bottom as a reviewer with a fixed checklist. The re-read is cheap and
the render is expensive — and re-reads catch the class that renders
find late, because most write-time defects are skipped decisions with
readable signatures:

- which states are unstyled (hover/focus/active/disabled/filled/empty)
- what each text element does at 2× length and at empty (wrap
  decisions made in spec vs discoveries left for the camera)
- what defaults to a native control or browser primitive
- what is a placeholder — invented chrome, unicode-as-icon, a control
  that exists nowhere in the house
- structural orphans — CSS rules for elements that don't exist,
  `display` set on elements carrying `[hidden]`, inline elements
  given width/height, unbalanced nesting
- **invisible-by-default styling** — `::-webkit-scrollbar`,
  `::selection`, `cursor`, `:focus-visible` rings, and any browser-
  rendered chrome the surface touches. These never appear in an
  element plan because they're invisible until wrong — that is
  exactly why they ship unstyled. If the checklist doesn't name
  them, they don't exist.
- **the plain-render pass** — describe each element in literal
  language with design vocabulary banned: "rounded box, border,
  arrow over number, muted text." If the plain description sounds
  like a default template, it is one — vocabulary ("shaped
  affordance", "composed region") must not be allowed to launder
  what the plain words expose.
- **the bootstrap test, per element** — would this element be
  indistinguishable inside a default template/tutorial? One
  question, no hedging. That is the user's detector — a pass that
  never runs it is reviewing a different artifact than the user sees.
- **the liveness check** — every rendered interactive element
  (button, tab, input, link, toggling class) gets asked "does it do
  anything?" — read the wiring, not the shape. A control with no
  handler, no state change, and no declared boundary is a defect at
  write time, not a capture-time surprise. Dead controls wear real
  control shapes; the lie is invisible until clicked.
- **the resolved-value check** — for every `--app-*` token an
  authored rule depends on, resolve it (`getComputedStyle` at the
  element) before the first screenshot. Binding a token's *name*
  proves nothing when a bundle redefines it on a stronger selector —
  an accent that resolves pink instead of blue is invisible until
  probed, and a blanket `button{}` reset silently beating a real
  `@layer` rule is the same class: cascade collisions don't appear
  in source, they appear in resolved values.
- and **walk it in the user's eye-order** — top-left down, logo and
  chrome first — not in build order. Scrutiny that follows
  authorship order under-checks exactly the elements the user sees
  first.

Findings are written before the camera opens — a read-back that
produces no artifact didn't run. The camera verifies the read-back;
it doesn't replace it.

### Pass 1 — Critique (before any fix)

- Run **before** implementation or as the first step of a fix task.
- **Fresh eyes required when reviewing your own work.** The reviewer must
  not be filtering for "did my change appear." When the subject is work
  this session (or your prior work) produced, fresh eyes means an actual
  second agent — a subagent given only the screenshots + the design
  question (it never saw your diff — that is the point). Self-review of
  self-produced work cannot be comprehensive: the reviewer shares the
  author's blind spot, so the "dedicated pass as a stranger" option exists
  only for surfaces you did not author (`15-analysis-depth.mdc` §7).
  When a subagent critiques, re-verify a sample of its findings against the
  live surface yourself (`06-subagent-gatecheck.mdc`) — its output seeds
  the artifact, it is not itself evidence.
- **Independent eyes run at three points, not one.** Post-spec (does the
  ladder/referent read right — a wrong register caught at spec is a
  five-line fix), post-concept (which reads strongest to a stranger —
  would a member pick it over the referent?), and post-build (the
  artifact review). The earlier the eyes run the cheaper the correction;
  eyes that only arrive at paint turn spec-time errors into rebuilds.
  A concept set where the eyes run only once gets one verdict for the
  set — per-concept verdicts or the concept axis was never sampled.
  Every eyes brief must carry the comparative question — "which would a
  member pick *over the referent*" — not only "find defects": defect
  enumeration measures the floor; the referent comparison measures the
  bar. A brief without it produces mechanical findings and aesthetic
  silence. And the brief must demand the altitude data the schema
  carries: a per-surface **score out of 10**, the **named distance to
  the bar** ("what would the 9/10 version of this surface have that
  this doesn't"), and per surface with an exemplar capture a
  **side-by-side compare** at the same viewport. A brief that asks
  "first three things wrong" commissions defect-hunting and gets
  defect-hunting — the lake-alto pass found 13 defects and 5 quality
  rows because that is what it was told to look for.
- **The first render gets its own eyes pass on generative work.** When
  the spec ran `## Directions` (new surface, mockup, redesign), the
  FIRST surface's first screenshots go to a stranger cold-read BEFORE
  the pattern propagates to siblings — deposited as an eyes-v2 artifact
  at `.unlazy/eyes/<sid>-first-render.md`, briefed against the spec's
  `## Quality contract` (a verdict vs the bar — distinctive moves
  landed, generic failure modes absent — not a defect sweep), and
  cited in this artifact. The author cannot cold-read its own
  template-read; first render is the cheapest point it can be caught.
  (The gate credits the pass by *cite*, not by filename — any eyes-v2
  artifact named in the review as the first-render pass counts.)
- **The spec itself gets the first stranger pass on generative work.**
  Before the first render — `.unlazy/eyes/<sid>-spec.md` — a stranger
  critiques the spec's *decisions* against the class ceiling
  (13-design-spec → Spec critique). Ceiling-limiting decisions are
  caught while they are still rows, not after they are eight pages.
- **Eyes coverage spans the product, not the first page.** The union of
  `## Coverage` rows across all of a session's eyes passes must touch
  every `## Surfaces` row before the loop can end — a surface no
  stranger ever saw is a surface no one reviewed. A launched pass that
  never deposited is not coverage; the gate reconciles launches vs
  deposited artifacts.
- **Per-element drill-down.** The review walks every `## Inventory`
  element row and gives each an *execution verdict* — not "present"
  but "is this as good as the best version of this element" — with
  render evidence or `DECLARED`. Effort becomes enumerable at element
  granularity; "the card exists" is not the same job as "the card is
  well-made." `## Element drill-down` carries the walk, at ≥ceil(
  inventory rows / 3) rows.
- **Per-page center-of-gravity.** Every surface in the review gets
  its own gravity question — what is THIS page for, and does its
  shell serve that eye-anchor? The template-sameness failure (one
  3-column shell stamped on every page regardless of the page's job)
  survives per-element checks because each element is fine in place;
  it is only visible when the page is asked what it is for. A context
  rail beside a single-thread conversation, a settings card eating a
  third of a chat — these are gravity violations, not element
  defects. Findings land class `aesthetic-floor`.
- **The interrogation and the two altitude verdicts.** `## Interrogation`
  carries one row per component — the expandable question ("could this be
  made better" → the verdict: does it expand, what does it earn) or
  `sole — <why>` on the surface. The review closes on two verdict lines:
  `holistic:` — the whole read at composition zoom — and `micro:` — the
  close read per element. A verdict at only one altitude is the half-read
  the interrogation exists to catch.
- **Scores make "fine" visible.** Every eyes pass deposits `## Scores`
  — per surface a 1–10 and the named distance-to-bar. Verdicts without
  numbers let "adequate" cover a 5 and an 8 with the same word.
- **Judge like the user does:** open it, click what a user would click,
  and ask of *every element* — does this make sense, is it easy to use, is
  it impossible to make this better. The member-facing site (`ui/features`,
  `ui/design`) is the reference bar for look and interaction quality —
  not Old, not "the desk already did it this way."
- **Enumerate, don't browse.** Coverage is a table, not a memory: one row
  per surface × viewport **× persona where permissions differ**, listing
  the elements actually looked at
  (every interactive control, every empty/loading/error/filled state,
  every dialog/inspector/menu the surface can open). "I looked at it" is
  not coverage; a row naming what was examined is. Where an owner and a
  minimal-permission member see different controls, both personas get
  rows — founder-powerful browsing verifies nothing about member
  experience.
- **Cells are tasks.** Where the surface exists to let someone do
  something, a coverage row records a *completed task* — "member with
  only RSVP permission signs up," "guest opens the share link" — not
  merely the elements present. A row that only attests existence is the
  presence-verdict failure in a new costume
  (`12-verification.mdc` → feature-level verification).
- **Predict before you look.** Each rendered cell gets its predicted
  state written in `## Predictions` before the capture — one line per
  cell. A prediction-observation difference is a finding the moment it
  appears; without a written prediction there is no observation that can
  fail.
- **Cover the whole surface, not the top viewport.** Pages and sheets
  longer than the viewport get captured at every scroll offset — scroll
  down, shoot, repeat until the bottom is on camera. Detail cards with
  internal scroll (`.app-mk-detail-body`), long tables, and multi-section
  forms are the usual misses: an element that lives below the fold and
  never appears in a shot was never reviewed. Lazy-loading lists get
  reviewed at both the first page and a loaded-more state.
- **The naive-viewer pass — interrogate, don't recognize.** Reading a
  capture as confirmation of what you believe it contains is not
  review ("that's the real component" — said of a box whose arrow
  paints transparent). Interrogate each region as a stranger, in
  writing: *what is this? what can I do here? where is the action?*
  An element a stranger can't explain (an unlabeled colored bar) or
  can't reach (a CTA stranded outside the reading column's scan
  path) is a P1. Comprehension and reachability are the user's axes;
  a review that only checks conformance to spec cannot see them.
- **Cold read before confirm read — always, and first.** Before ANY
  list is consulted — the fix list, the spec, the coverage rows — the
  reviewer looks at each capture and writes the first three things
  wrong with it, from the eye only — including the boundaries: content
  at every container edge and chrome seam, anything clipped, occluded,
  or colliding is a named first-glance class, not confirm-read detail.
  Then the confirm-read (the
  checklist, the findings list, the spec diff) runs. The order is
  the protocol: a review that opens with the list sees only what the
  list came to check — dead space, thin composition, underwhelming
  regions are visually loud at first glance and invisible to a
  fix-verification scan. The artifact's `## Cold read` section
  carries the first-glance wrongs; `## Findings` carries what the
  instruments then confirmed. A review that ran confirm-read only
  never looked at the surface — it looked at its own paperwork.
- **Pixel verification, not DOM verification.** The verdict cites
  what is *painted*, not what is in the tree: a 40×56 box with
  `flex-direction:column` and an invisible arrow passes every DOM
  check and fails the eye. Element-crop the authored components and
  describe what is actually painted — geometry is not paint.
- **Provenance tri-classification (match / real / identical asks).**
  Every chrome region is classified in the artifact `## Provenance`
  as `real-dumped` / `approximated` / `invented`, with its source.
  Invented classes under real-sounding names is provenance
  laundering — a violation, not a "fallback". "The chrome IS the
  real thing" is an overclaim the moment one invented node exists.
- **Screenshots are evidence, never the whole audit.** A shot shows what
  *did* render in one state; the source shows what *can* render in every
  state. Every critiqued surface also gets a code pass: read the paint
  code for the states no shot can hold (hover/focus/disabled, loading,
  error, empty, permission-gated, data-permutation variants), check the
  event wiring actually does what the affordance promises, and reason
  about what the element *should* be — not just whether the one captured
  state looks right. "It looked fine in the screenshot" is not a verdict
  on the element.
- **A user-reported defect is a finding the moment it is named — and it
  fires TWO findings.** When the user describes a UI/UX problem, it
  enters the findings ledger (and the todo list) at once with severity
  assigned — it does not wait for the next scheduled pass, it is never
  absorbed silently into ongoing work, and it never leaves the session
  in a "residual" or "known issues" section unfixed unless the user has
  seen it named and accepted deferring it. Missing an item the user
  already reported is a worse failure than missing one they haven't.
  **The second finding is on the instrument that passed it:** a user
  catch is external evidence that an internal pass failed — the same
  round names which lens/vocabulary/comparison should have caught it
  and patches that instrument, not only the artifact. Patching the
  surface while leaving the blind instrument in place is how the same
  blind spot survives every fix round — the user's rejection is the
  strongest review signal the system has; treating it as a ticket
  instead of a diagnosis is the waste.
- **Defect classes get enumerated, not sampled.** A bare "find defects"
  pass produces whichever class samples loudest — one review found
  sixteen text-space findings and zero geometry ones because nobody
  asked about geometry. Pass 1 walks the classes by name, each with a
  verdict or `DECLARED:` — geometry & boundary collisions (content
  clipped at container edges, sliding under chrome, controls cut by
  `overflow:hidden`), environment coverage (the capture matrix
  contains the user's actual viewport — a surface verified at 1400
  for a 4K user was verified where nobody looks), omission vs the
  domain checklist (what should exist but doesn't — the class
  presence-checks cannot see), responsive behavior, interaction /
  liveness, accessibility, truthfulness / provenance, state
  transitions, visual hierarchy & edge collisions. Enumerating the
  classes is what separates "checked" from "looked".
- **Craft findings are mandatory, not optional.** A findings ledger
  containing only mechanical/behavioral defects (wraps, inert
  controls, data mismatches) and zero visual-quality findings is a
  thin pass — the user's detector is perceptual ("this looks
  un-designed"), and a review that surfaces nothing perceptual
  reviewed mechanics, not the artifact. Every authored element gets
  the bootstrap test and the plain-render description; every region
  gets the element-level referent comparison — property by property
  against the real source (`file:line` or capture), not a vibe
  comparison. "Resembles the exemplar" is not a comparison; naming
  the differing px/weight/treatment is.
- **Prior critiques are the regression set.** Every defect the user
  has ever reported in this session is ground truth for the next
  review — a fresh look that surfaces none of the previously
  reported defect *classes* (un-themed browser chrome, generic
  controls, un-composed headers, approximated house chrome) missed
  them, and that gap is itself a finding. Re-reporting a class the
  user already named is the worst failure on this list.
- **The structural ratchet — findings against an anatomy count as a
  verdict on the anatomy.** Every finding carries the anatomy it
  accuses (row-unit, header region, column scheme, hero block — the
  structural unit, not the CSS class). Three or more findings against
  one anatomy, across ANY review rounds, put the anatomy itself back
  on the table: the next move is re-derivation from the referent, not
  a fourth patch. Per-finding patching ratifies the structure it
  fixes — a patch series that looks like progress while the form
  stays wrong is sunk-cost ratcheting, and the ledger counts it as
  coverage.
- **Severity:** P0 blocking / P1 quality / P2 polish / P3 preference
  (`taste-design-review`). P3s are listed, not fixed, in the same pass.
- **Design-review findings have exactly six states — there is no "residual":**
  `OPEN` (work in progress — keeps the session going), `FIXED`,
  `VERIFIED` (fixed and re-painted/re-probed), `BLOCKED: <a real
  external wall — the row stays open and holds the turn until the work
  lands or `STOP: BLOCKED`/`STOP: USER-GATE` is declared>`,
  `ACCEPTED: <the user re-scoped it — their verb on the row>`,
  `NOT-A-DEFECT: <why it was never broken — the examined-and-correct
  terminal for a suspected finding>`. Bare `accepted`/`blocked` with no
  reason is not a state. `QUEUED:`/`SKIPPED:`/`later` are not states —
  an open row is worked, never rescheduled.
  These six are the surface-class set — a design defect is fixed on
  the render or it stays open; `FILED:` does not exist here because
  filing a design defect is deferral. The canonical findings-
  disposition vocabulary (the doc-class `FILED:`/`CARRIED:` set
  included) lives at `15-analysis-depth.mdc` item 15.
  The words *residual, known issues, nice-to-have, for later, future
  work, out of scope* are banned in artifacts and reports — they are
  closure framing: unfinished work wearing a finished label. The stop
  gate blocks them.
- **A pass ends when the ledger ends, not when the turn feels long.**
  Open P0/P1 findings mean the work continues — fix, or name a real
  blocker to the user. "I found more while working" never ends the pass;
  it *extends* the ledger. Deferral belongs to the user, never to the
  agent: if you want to leave something open, ask.
- Invoke the design skills first (`08-code-quality.mdc` → Design and
  visual work): `design-taste`, `taste-design-review`, plus the
  specialists as they apply — read `SKILL.md` files directly if the skill
  tool doesn't list them.

### Appearance-class work — the outcome → exemplar → kind protocol

Two documented failure moves force this protocol:

1. **Email-preview zoom.** Ask: preview "closer to the standard size of an
   actual email". Fix: `zoom` on the existing thumbnail — bigger box, same
   miniature. The dimension check passed; the look was wrong.
2. **Email-as-card.** Ask: "improve the design of the email that's sent
   out". Three turns polished the inherited card chrome (border, radius,
   added a brand rule) — until the user named it: "emails are not cards".
   The ask's enumerable items were all satisfied; the goal ("a good
   email") was never in the objective. Postmortem: generation sampled the
   *corpus prior* (template-builder markup is what "HTML email" looks
   like in training data), self-consistency locked the first sample, and
   no step compared the artifact to a real product email.

Both share one mechanism: **the real goal was dropped at decomposition
and a measurable proxy took its place.** This protocol keeps the goal
alive from first sentence to final verdict. It fires on **any
appearance-class ask** — "improve", "design", "better", "polish",
"looks", "UI/UX", or any user-reported visual defect — not just analogy
vocabulary.

- **The outcome sentence comes first — before decomposition, before any
  edit, and visible to the user.** For an appearance ask, the turn's
  first output states it: *"you want X to read/feel like Y"* where Y is
  a class of real artifact ("a letter from the product", "an inbox
  render at normal scale"). The user's enumerated asks (logo, no pink,
  better buttons) are **constraints on that goal — never the goal**.
  A checklist can impersonate an unstated goal; it cannot impersonate a
  stated one. A wrong reading caught in that sentence costs the user a
  two-second scan, not three turns of artifacts.
- **Exemplar conditioning before generation.** Name 2–4 real exemplars
  of the artifact class and extract their *shared structural rules* —
  not their styling (Linear/Stripe/GitHub mail → "flat column, no card
  chrome, small logo, dominant headline, one CTA"). Unconditioned
  generation samples the corpus prior, which for most HTML artifacts is
  template sludge; the exemplar list is what replaces that prior. Local
  referents count (the member surfaces, the redesign mockups) — and
  when none exists, the named exemplar must be external, named anyway.
  This happens **while** code is being written, not audited after —
  self-consistency anchors on the first artifact that exists.
- **The kind-check: artifact class before styling.** State what kind of
  thing is being built ("a letter", "a notice strip", "a real-scale
  render") and whether the current implementation already has that kind
  right. A document gets typography, whitespace and one accent — never
  container chrome; reaching for border/radius/shadow/a colored strip on
  something a user *reads* is the tell that a control is being styled,
  and it triggers the kind-check, not a style decision. If the kind is
  wrong, the first change is **structural** — and styling a kind-wrong
  artifact is classified as wasted work, not progress.
- **Every element must name its job — or it is a finding.** In the
  critique and in the verify pass, each element gets one sentence of
  purpose. Ornament cannot name a job; that single test mechanically
  catches decorative chrome (top rules, redundant separators, fake page
  furniture) that pure property-checks wave through.
- **The divergence list closes the loop.** Verification against an
  appearance goal includes a written list: *"how does this differ from
  the named exemplar, element by element — and is each difference
  deliberate?"* Every divergence is justified in writing or it is an
  open finding. This is the taste pass made falsifiable — it cannot be
  satisfied by "no console errors". (When a design-spec governs the
  change, the list lives in the artifact's `## Deltas` section —
  `13-design-spec.mdc`.)
- **Walk the surface like its designer — the whole-surface
  interrogation.** Per-element checks miss emergent failures. Stand
  back on the render and answer, in words: where does the eye land
  first — is that the right place? Does the surface cohere under one
  concept, or read as a committee of compliant parts? What did I add
  that earns nothing? What state is missing? These are questions a
  working designer runs every pass — the method is the quality
  (`13-design-spec.mdc` → the stance), and an unanswered question is a
  finding, not a shrug.
- **Subtraction counts as a decision — report it like one.** Deletions
  score low on visible-work proxies, which is why the system drifts
  toward adding furniture. Report removals as first-class design
  decisions with their rationale ("removed card chrome — emails are
  letters") at the same weight as additions. A small diff accompanied
  by named removals reads as designed, not thin.
- **Appearance work is not done without the comparison.** Visual work
  closes only when the exemplar comparison exists with no unexplained
  divergences — the taste pass is a completion condition, not optional
  polish on top of mechanical checks.

#### The referent rule (unchanged — the foundation of this protocol)

- **Analogy vocabulary means the spec lives outside the codebase.** When
  a request describes an element by comparison to a real-world thing —
  "real", "actual", "standard", "looks like", "like Mailchimp", "a real
  email" — the spec is the referent, not the replica in front of you.
  Preview mocks, thumbnails, and canned demos exist to *imitate* a real
  surface; a request about their size or fidelity is about the thing
  imitated.
- **Referent identification before any mechanism.** One written line
  before the first edit — "the user's X = <the real thing their words
  point at>" — in the artifact's Interpretation block. If no referent
  can be named, or two readings lead to materially different
  implementations (magnify vs. re-lay-out), ask. That is an
  interpretation question; "don't ask, just do it" instructions govern
  permission and never cover interpretation.
- **Magnification is not sizing.** `zoom` and `transform: scale()`
  change the raster, not the layout metrics — internal typography stays
  miniature while the box grows. Reaching for a magnifier on a
  sizing/fidelity request is the signal that a geometric reading beat a
  referential one; stop and re-check the referent first. Legitimate
  uses exist (real zoom controls) — the tripwire is whether size is
  being "fixed" by enlarging a shrunken replica.
- **Appearance findings close on worded verdicts, never numbers.** A
  bounding box cannot distinguish "renders at real scale" from
  "magnified thumbnail" — both measure the same. The finding closes with
  a verdict that quotes the user's phrasing and answers it in words:
  `user asked "closer to a standard email" → looks like: <sentence>`.
  A verdict that can't be filled honestly is an open finding.
- **Aesthetic work iterates — it never one-shots.** At least one full
  compare-to-referent loop (render → hold against the referent or a
  captured render of it → adjust) before an appearance finding can
  verify. If the real document the replica imitates can be rendered,
  render it side-by-side — that render, not house style, is the bar for
  preview-type surfaces.

### Pass 2 — Verify (after fixes)

- Repaint the fixed surfaces at every affected viewport **and every
  scroll offset the surface needs**, re-run the same enumeration, and
  update the artifact — findings resolved, regressed, or newly visible.
- **Shared-budget fixes re-verify the whole unit.** An edit inside a
  coupled allocation — colgroup, flex row, grid track, shared measure —
  re-shoots and re-reviews the entire unit, not the named symptom:
  subtracting from one term reallocates the others (a name-column fix
  starved the action column it never re-measured). The finding's
  closure row carries the siblings re-verified, not just the fix.
- A fix is not done because it renders. It is done when a fresh look at
  the whole element finds nothing left to flag at that severity bar —
  in the code pass as well as on camera.
- **Pass 2 is not the end either.** Findings that verify closed move the
  session forward into the remaining OPEN rows — you do not report a
  clean verify as the finish line while the ledger still has open items.
  The turn ends when every row is terminal, a real external wall exists
  and is declared as `STOP:`, or the user re-scoped the row themselves.

#### The composition verdict (appearance-class work)

Mechanical verification ("the fix is on camera") answers whether the
patch landed. It never answers the ask's actual question — *does the
surface look deliberately designed*. When the Interpretation names a
referent/exemplar (any appearance-class ask), the repaint is not done
until each repainted surface also gets a written **composition
verdict** — the chef tastes the food. Required, mechanically gated
(`10-rule-enforcement.mdc` → TASTE-VERDICT):

- **Describe the render as a stranger.** Two sentences minimum, in
  structural terms — where the eye lands first, what the dominant
  region is, what carries the hierarchy. "A grid of cards" or "rows of
  text" is itself a verdict. Adjectives alone are not a description:
  `clean`, `fine`, `good`, `polished`, `sleek`, `premium`, `modern`
  unaccompanied by structure are banned in verdict cells — the same
  anti-adjective doctrine as the audit ledger.
- **Name the weakest area.** Every repainted surface names its weakest
  visual area — "the hero slab is a flat rectangle with a floating
  date", "the right third of the content column is unused", "the
  comment section has no visual anchor". A weakest-cell of `none —
  <why>` is legal only with the defense written. A pass where every
  surface's weakest area is empty or hand-waved is a pass where the
  judging never happened — an all-clear across N repainted surfaces is
  itself a finding, because real surfaces always have a weakest part.
- **Map the exemplar's parts.** Before writing the verdict, extract
  the named exemplar's structural parts (hero band? purchase/action
  card? location block? weighted sections?) and check each for a
  counterpart on this surface. A part with no counterpart is a named
  divergence, not an invisible gap.
- **Emit the verdict token.** Each surface row ends `| AT-BAR |` or
  `| BELOW-BAR: <what it lacks — or the finding id it became> |`. A
  BELOW-BAR verdict is a P1/P2 finding: it joins the ledger like any
  other and the work continues.
- **Name the biggest lever.** Each verdict row also answers "the
  single highest-value improvement available on ANY axis — structure,
  content, interaction, functionality — what would most improve this
  whole?" For BELOW-BAR rows it names the finding; for AT-BAR rows
  it is the honest next-best thing (or `none — <defense>`). A
  whole-surface look that produces no lever is the skimmed-bird's-eye
  signature.
- **Re-ask the kind question at Pass 2.** The spec's kind-check fires
  before composition; the repaint gets it again: is this surface the
  right *kind* of thing — or a polished version of the wrong one?
  Anchoring on the first frame built is a documented failure mode;
  "better version of the wrong structure" is the trap it catches.
- **Describe before you verify.** Pass 2 opens with the whole-surface
  stranger-description BEFORE any fix-check — the open question
  ("what do I see") must be answered before the closed one ("is my
  fix there"), or the preloaded yes writes the observation.
- **Walk for absences — `## Absences`.** Enumeration finds
  wrong-things-present; it structurally cannot find
  right-things-absent (the missing cover art, the missing weighted
  region, the missing empty-state). Per repainted surface: what
  *should* exist here — per the exemplar's parts, per the task —
  that doesn't? The question is unfakeable only when it is
  comparative: each row names the real-world comparator it was held
  against (`vs <product/class>` — or `none — <defense>` when a
  surface genuinely has no referent). An absence answer with no named
  referent is a vibe, not a comparison. Each entry ends
  `RAISED: <finding id>` or `NOT-NEEDED: <defense>`.
- **The author's verdict needs an independent looker — and
  independence is procedural when no subagent runs.** Your own look
  at your own repaint can only confirm intent — the cook tasting the
  plate from memory of the recipe. Subagents cost rate limits; the
  same independence is achievable *in writing*: `## Fresh-look`
  carries a delta table — **predicted** (what each surface will show,
  written BEFORE the repaint exists) vs **observed** (what the shot
  actually shows, ≥10 words, structural) vs `MATCH | MISS: <what
  differed>`. A prediction written after the shots is memory wearing
  a blindfold — the two columns exist to keep the passes separated in
  the artifact, and ≥8 all-MATCH rows is the written-after signature
  (FRESH-LOOK is mechanically gated). A critique-briefed subagent —
  screenshots only, no diff, no intent — still satisfies it and is
  the strongest looker when one can be afforded; the user's own
  render review counts too (`EXEMPT-FRESH-EYES` names it).
  **Presence deltas are not quality review.** Predict→observe rows
  that only confirm "element rendered where expected" catch wrapping
  bugs and miss design failure — the same author writing both
  columns five minutes apart produces zero independence. Every
  Fresh-look table ends with three mandatory quality answers:
  `weakest area:` the surface's weakest region named honestly,
  `exemplar-cut:` the one thing the exemplar's author would remove,
  and `right-kind:` is this the right KIND of artifact vs the
  referent (the question that catches a Reddit-width feed built
  under a Canny-intent ask). A quality answer that finds nothing is
  the looker looking away.
- **The move-on gate — per-item closure.** Due diligence dies at the
  task transition, not the session end: item N is marked done and
  item N+1 begins with the scrutiny still unwritten. Before a todo
  may be marked `completed`, its `CLOSURE: <item stem> — <what was
  questioned across the axes, what was applied/raised, the verdict>`
  must already exist in `## Closures` — the PreToolUse hook
  (`check-item-closure.mjs`) refuses the transition mechanically, and
  the stop-gate (ITEM-CLOSURE) verifies completeness at the end.
  Scrutiny that isn't written before the transition didn't happen.
- **Answer the excellence question in writing — `## Raises`.** For
  each repainted surface, enumerate what the *best* version of it does
  that this one doesn't — the "what would make this an extremely good
  product" question made concrete. Every entry ends `RAISED: <finding
  id>` (it became work) or `DEFENDED: <why the bar doesn't require
  it>`. A surface with zero raises is a surface whose excellence was
  never considered; a section that names only cheap wins is the
  hand-waved variant — entries should name the expensive move, not
  the easy one.
- **Composition has measurable seams — use them.** Taste can't be
  automated, but its absence has signatures worth probing on the
  repaint: the largest flat uniform-color region vs. surface area (a
  >~30% slab with a single glyph inside is the empty-hero class);
  content bounding box vs. container width (a left-aligned cluster
  leaving dead space); the count of distinct visual weights on the
  page (one weight everywhere = flat). A probe that flags is a lead
  for the verdict, never the verdict itself — the row still closes on
  words.

#### The element scrutiny (all UI work — the second look)

Verdicts judge surfaces whole. They do not catch the element-level
default — "put down an input box and moved on" — because an element
can be individually un-styled inside a surface that reads fine. After
fixes land, every touched surface gets a **scrutiny pass** — the
"is there literally anything more I can do to this before moving on"
question asked *per element*, in writing (ELEMENT-SCRUTINY is
mechanically gated):

- Per element/region of the surface: question its placement (does its
  position make sense relative to its neighbors — does the eye arrive
  in the right order?), its size (does the measure carry the weight of
  its job vs the exemplar's equivalent?), and its finish (is it
  styled, or is it a native/default paint wearing tokens?). A
  `questioned` cell that names no actual question is the skipped-pass
  signature — the row must name what was doubted, not that looking
  happened.
- **Scrutiny is axis-complete, not design-only.** Design is one axis
  of five — every row tags its axis (`visual`/`functional`/
  `security`/`performance`/`quality`) and cites the evidence type that
  axis runs on: functional = an exercised state (probe/click/submit/
  observed render), security = a `file:line` citation or `n/a:` +
  reason, performance = a measured number or `n/a:` + reason, quality
  = what the diff re-read found, visual = the shot description. A
  missing axis gets `AXIS-NA: <axis> — <reason>` inside the section —
  a session that never wrote a security/performance/quality row on
  touched code checked the look and skipped the substance.
- Every row ends `APPLIED: <the improvement made>`, `DEFENDED: <why
  the element already holds — ≥5 words>`, or `RAISED: <finding id>`
  (the doubt is real and became a finding — e.g. on a BELOW-BAR
  surface whose fix is in the ledger). APPLIED rows are the
  pass's own output: a scrutiny pass that finds zero improvements
  across every element on a reworked surface is the same
  never-judged signature as an all-clear verdict — expect some
  APPLIED entries, and record them as the design decisions they are.
- This is the pre-close re-examination, not a repeat of Pass 1:
  Pass 1 asks what is wrong, scrutiny asks *what else can be better*.
  An element that survives scrutiny earns "done" — an element never
  scrutinized was passed, not finished.
- **Coverage derives from the DOM, not memory.** A hand-written
  element list can silently skip the element that needed questioning —
  the untouched-element blind spot is *authored* blindness. Run
  `.devin/scripts/ui-coverage-probe.mjs` against the touched surfaces;
  it dumps every interactive element, section, and the state counts no
  screenshot enumerates (hidden/disabled/aria-pressed/details/inputs)
  per viewport × scroll offset — read-only, it never clicks. Cite it
  as `coverage-source: .unlazy/coverage/<sid>.json` in the artifact
  (COVERAGE-EXTERNAL is mechanically gated — coverage enumerated
  <30% of the DOM's interactive count is the skip signature).
- **User-caught misses are calibration, not just fixes.** Every defect
  the user reports is a gate failure — some check should have caught
  it and didn't. Each gets a `CALIBRATION: <the miss> → <the check
  that should have fired> → <the rule change made>` row in
  `## Calibration` (mechanically gated whenever rejection-tracker
  recorded a report). Your catches become rule inputs permanently.
- **The liveness ledger — mechanical, not judgment.** Every rendered
  interactive on the surface gets a `## Liveness` row: `handler
  wired` / `state changes` / `boundary declared` / `dead`. A control
  with none of the first three is a defect — countable, no taste
  needed. The coverage probe reports `deadCandidates` (elements
  whose DOM shape has no action signature); the ledger must account
  for every one.
- **Causes are traced, not narrated.** A finding's root cause is
  cited to `file:line` before it is reported — "the rule lives in a
  file we didn't link" asserted while the real file sat ungrepped is
  a wrong cause with a confident voice. `probably`, `likely`,
  `somewhere`, `almost certainly` in an analysis row is a defect in
  the analysis itself.
- **The standing defect taxonomy is the regression set.** The
  accumulated user-reported defect classes (§ The defect taxonomy,
  end of this file) are re-walked at every fresh look: a pass that
  misses a class the user already named is the worst failure, worse
  than missing a new one.

